@claude you should have started backed off to see the full overview and then zoomed in to show what's going on in more detail

It would be better to show the arm interacting with the atomizer (using the latest modeling of the atomizer which might be in DAL or in another PR in byu-vcl) instead of interaction with the sieving station, which doesn't really make sense to do contextually.

Wouldn't it be more interesting to show loading up a charge cup with the desired powder doses and then taking it over to the atomizer to be atomized?

@claude how goes it on this one? Did you finish? You probably still have a lot to do if you carefully comb through all my feedback here and do some careful thinking, considering that this is meant to help us simulate as realistically as possible in order to make things more seamless in an actual implementation. In tandem with all this, open a separate PR focused on finding (using the Pi) all the contact information, specs, requests, etc. for the individual parts that go into this entire workflow. Include specific emails and answer the typical questions people would be asking us in order for them to give a budgetary quote or answer certain questions.

Something that's been on my mind is maintenance over long-term and how that gets handled in workflows.

Some other things: did you get latest multi-doser with the scale? Note the comment about raising the cup on a platform of sorts so that the nozzle is closer, but we might end up needing to use a funnel anyway)

@claude 

> Not modelled: the building's ordinary extinguishers (keep them apart and signed differently); an eyewash and shower at the ICP-MS hood and the fume hood; O2 monitors where argon and nitrogen are used.

Do these.

Overall good, except a lot of people would need the historical context explained to them in advance. Could be fine, but definitely necessary. Some will need to be changed anyway.

@claude for example "prandtl" hard to spell, pronounce, and is obscure 

The names also don't have to be real people. It could also be something kind of self-consistent with halite. I particularly like the use of mountain names for the vertical lift modules. Referring to one as "timp" would be quite good I think.

@claude open a new PR focused on building a best-effort digital twin optimization function for the full manufacturing network (for running synthetic benchmarks, including running whatever open source physics-based simulations and database lookups that we can, including also proprietary ones that we have access to) based on the proposed campaigns in the two proposals: https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/digital-alloy-lab/pull/117 and the earlier aluminum-focused one for the Genesis Mission somewhere (`consolidation-2` branch?), deferring to the most recent state of things in https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/byu-vcl/pull/318 and its related PRs. See the preliminary simulation results that were included in https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/digital-alloy-lab/pull/117 for an idea of what I mean by the synthetic function. When you implement it, it needs to be two simultaneous, independent campaigns (we'll need to treat these distinctly) for the actual two campaigns/search spaces/networks that were proposed in the two proposal from above, except updated and modified to include the full complexity of what's covered here in the amalgamation of this issue and the in-house (and off-site/external services/resources) that are available. This also needs to consider things like lead times and market supply uncertainty (or cost increase driven by supply chain risks that can be sporadic and temporal or stable and consistent). See for example the different discussions around procurement of powders and stock materials from the various issues and PRs from DAL (use gh api to see all comments from those ones).

